4.4 Explanation of the application
1.	The system asks the user to enter the number of loyalty years.
2.	It validates the input to ensure that the number is at least 1.
3.	An array named rewards stores the points for each year.
4.	The first two years are initialized to 1 point each.
5.	A loop calculates the points for the remaining years using the Fibonacci formula.
6.	Another loop displays the points for each year.
4.5 Benefits to the company
•	Efficiency: Each year's reward is calculated once.
•	Data storage: The array makes it easy to access a customer's reward points by year.
•	Scalability: The system can support many customers by processing each customer's loyalty duration.
•	Maintainability: The reward calculation is simple and easy to update.
•	Extensibility: The system can later integrate customer IDs, databases, reward redemption, and reporting.
For a production system, the company should also consider integer overflow, input validation, data persistence, and whether Fibonacci rewards remain financially sustainable as customers accumulate years of loyalty.
