5.1 Expected performance
All approaches calculate the 30th Fibonacci number, which is 832040 under the standard definition \(F(0)=0,\ F(1)=1\).
Method	Time complexity	Expected performance at \(n=30\)
Recursive	Exponential	Slowest
Iterative array	Linear	Very fast
Memoization	Linear	Very fast
Tabulation	Linear	Very fast
The basic recursive implementation performs roughly 2.7 million function calls for \(n=30\), whereas iterative and dynamic programming implementations calculate the sequence using only about 30 loop iterations. Exact operation counts vary with the implementation.
5.2 Measuring actual execution times in Java
Execution times depend on the computer, Java version, JVM optimization, and measurement method. The following benchmark can be added to a Java project to compare the approaches on the same machine.

import java.util.Arrays;

public class FibonacciBenchmark {

    static volatile int resultSink;

    static int recursive(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return n;
        }
        return recursive (n - 1) + recursive(n - 2);
    }

    static int[] iterative (int n) {
        int[] fib = new int[n + 1];

        if (n >= 1) {
            fib [1] = 1;
        }

        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            fib[i] = fib [i - 1] + fib [i - 2];
        }

        return fib;
    }

    static int [] memoized(int n) {
        int [] memo = new int [n + 1];
        Arrays.fill(memo, -1);

        memo [0] = 0;
        if (n >= 1) {
            memo [1] = 1;
        }

        memoHelper(n, memo);
        return memo;
    }

    static int memoHelper (int n, int [] memo) {
        if (memo[n] != -1) {
            return memo[n];
        }

        memo[n] = memoHelper(n - 1, memo)
                + memoHelper(n - 2, memo);

        return memo[n];
    }

    static long measure(String method, int repeats) {
        int n = 30;

        // Warm up the JVM.
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            resultSink = recursive(n);
            resultSink = iterative(n)[n];
            resultSink = memoized(n)[n];
        }

        long start = System.nanoTime();

        for (int i = 0; i < repeats; i++) {
            switch (method) {
                case "Recursive":
                    resultSink = recursive(n);
                    break;
                case "Iterative":
                    resultSink = iterative(n)[n];
                    break;
                case "Memoization":
                    resultSink = memoized(n)[n];
                    break;
            }
        }

        return (System.nanoTime() - start) / repeats;
    }

    public static void main (String [] args) {
        int repeats = 1000;

        System.out.println("Average time per operation at n = 30");

        System.out.println("Recursive: "
                + measure ("Recursive", repeats) + " ns");

        System.out.println("Iterative: "
                + measure ("Iterative", repeats) + " ns");

        System.out.println("Memoization: "
                + measure ("Memoization", repeats) + " ns");
    }
}
The program reports average execution time in nanoseconds per operation. Run it on your computer and record the results for your assignment.
Note: This is a basic classroom benchmark, not a rigorous microbenchmark. For reliable results, use a Java benchmarking framework such as JMH, which accounts for JVM warm-up and other measurement effects. The exact measured values should come from your own run rather than being invented in advance.
