6. Recommendation and Conclusion
The company should use the iterative approach with arrays, which also implements the bottom-up principle of dynamic programming.
The reasons are:
1.	Fast execution: It has \(O(n)\) time complexity.
2.	Predictable memory usage: It requires \(O(n)\) space to store the reward sequence.
3.	No recursive overhead: It avoids repeated function calls and stack overflow risks from deep recursion.
4.	Easy retrieval: Each year's reward can be accessed directly from the array.
5.	Suitable for growth: The system can process loyalty records for thousands of customers without the exponential cost of basic recursion.
For larger inputs, the company should use long or Big Integer, depending on the maximum reward values. It should also test the reward policy against its budget before applying it to real customers.
In conclusion, although recursion is useful for demonstrating how the Fibonacci sequence works, iteration and dynamic programming provide much better performance. An iterative array-based solution is the most straightforward choice for this customer loyalty reward system because it is efficient, easy to understand, and well suited to generating rewards for every year of customer loyalty.

